  "body_mappings": [
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        498.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "In the first ten days of September 1854, cholera ravaged an overcrowded corner of London's Soho; upwards of 500 people perished. Most doctors attributed epidemics to miasma, foul air arising from filth. John Snow, a physician who had been convinced since 1849 that cholera's poison was swallowed, not inhaled, suspected the Broad Street pump."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        410.96141732283473,
        461.8976377952756,
        490.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Finding little visible impurity, he hesitated, then consulted the death register and questioned bereaved families. Most victims lived near the pump and drank from it. At his urging, parish officials removed the handle, but daily fatal attacks had already plunged from a peak of 143 to 12. He later mapped the deaths."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        343.0614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        402.46141732283473
      ],
      "text": "In 1855 Edmund Parkes, a professor of clinical medicine, interpreted the cluster on that map as a poison drifting through the air. Yet a map could chart where people died, not what they drank."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        255.3614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        334.5614173228347
      ],
      "text": "The crucial evidence, gathered in 1854, lay in exceptions. Amid the deaths in the surrounding streets, a workhouse with its own well lost a mere five of 535 inmates. More than 70 nearby brewery workers, with a beer allowance and their own well, escaped severe cholera."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        187.4614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        246.8614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Conversely, a Hampstead widow who had not set foot in Soho for months had the pump's water carted to her daily. She died, as did a visiting niece who drank it. Hampstead then had no cholera."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        99.7614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        178.9614173228347
      ],
      "text": "In 1855 a government inquiry declined to condemn the pump, contending its water had at most absorbed airborne poison. Meanwhile, the sceptical curate Henry Whitehead set out to refute Snow but found that those who drank from the pump were far more likely to succumb to the disease than others, vindicating him."
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        517.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "1854年9月上旬，霍乱在伦敦苏豪区一个拥挤不堪的角落肆虐；超过/多于500人丧生。多数医生把流行病归因于瘴气，也就是从污秽中产生的、污浊的空气。约翰·斯诺，一位内科医生，自1849年起就确信霍乱的毒素是吞进去的，而不是吸进去的，他怀疑问题出在宽街那口水泵上。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        429.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        509.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "在泵水里没发现多少肉眼可见的杂质，他一时犹豫，随后查阅了死亡登记册，又去询问痛失亲人的家庭。罹难者大多住在这口泵附近，也喝过它的水。在他的敦促下，教区官员卸下了泵柄，可这时每天新发的致死病例早已从143例的峰值骤降到12例。他后来把死亡病例标在了地图上。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        360.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        420.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "1855年，埃德蒙·帕克斯，一位临床医学教授，看到那张地图上死者扎堆，便把这种聚集解读为空气中飘散的毒素所致。可地图只能绘制/记录人死在哪里，绘制/记录不了他们喝了什么。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        272.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        352.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "至关重要的/决定性的证据早在1854年就已收集到，就在于那些例外/个例中。在周围的街巷死者接连不断的期间内，一所自有水井的济贫院（收容贫民的机构）里，535名被收容者只死了区区五人。附近一家啤酒厂的70多名工人有啤酒津贴，厂里也有自己的水井，都逃过了重症霍乱。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        203.66141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        263.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "反过来，汉普斯特德的一位寡妇已有几个月没踏进过苏豪，却让人把这口泵的水每天用车运来。她死了，来探望她并喝了这水的侄女也死了。汉普斯特德那时并没有霍乱。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        115.16141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        195.16141732283472
      ],
      "text": "1855年，政府的调查委员会拒绝给这口泵定罪，坚称泵水至多是吸收了经空气传播的毒素。与此同时，心存怀疑的副牧师（协助教区牧师的低级神职人员）亨利·怀特黑德一心想驳倒斯诺，却发现喝泵水的人死于这种病的可能性远高于旁人，反倒证明了斯诺是正确的。"
    }
  ],
